an9.38:5.6 | S: Tastes known by the tongue … | T: flavors cognizable via the tongue…
an9.38:5.7 | S: Touches known by the body, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: tactile sensations cognizable via the body—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked to sensual desire.
an9.38:5.8 | S: These five kinds of sensual stimulation are called the world in the training of the Noble One. | T: These are the five strings of sensuality that, in the discipline of the noble ones, are called the cosmos.
an9.38:6.1 | S: Take a mendicant who, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters and remains in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: “There is the case where a monk—quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities—enters & remains in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation.
an9.38:6.2 | S: This is called a mendicant who, having gone to the end of the world, meditates at the end of the world. | T: This is called a monk who, coming to the end of the cosmos, remains at the end of the cosmos.
an9.38:6.3 | S: Others say of them: | T: Others say of him,
an9.38:6.4 | S: ‘They’re included in the world, and haven’t yet left the world.’ | T: ‘He is encompassed in the cosmos; he has not escaped from the cosmos.’
an9.38:6.5 | S: And I also say this: | T: And I too say of him,
an9.38:6.6 | S: ‘They’re included in the world, and haven’t yet left the world.’ | T: ‘He is encompassed in the cosmos; he has not escaped from the cosmos.’
an9.38:7.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, as the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, enters and remains in the second absorption … | T: [Similarly with the second,
an9.38:7.2 | S: third absorption … | T: third,
an9.38:7.3 | S: fourth absorption. | T: & fourth jhānas,
an9.38:7.4 | S: This is called a mendicant who, having gone to the end of the world, meditates at the end of the world. | T: (none)
an9.38:7.5 | S: Others say of them: | T: (none)
an9.38:7.6 | S: ‘They’re included in the world, and haven’t yet left the world.’ | T: (none)
an9.38:7.7 | S: And I also say this: | T: (none)
an9.38:7.8 | S: ‘They’re included in the world, and haven’t yet left the world.’ | T: (none)
an9.38:8.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, going totally beyond perceptions of form, with the disappearance of perceptions of impingement, not focusing on perceptions of diversity, aware that ‘space is infinite’, enters and remains in the dimension of infinite space. | T: and with the attainment of the dimensions of the infinitude of space,
an9.38:8.2 | S: This is called a mendicant who, having gone to the end of the world, meditates at the end of the world. | T: (none)
an9.38:8.3 | S: Others say of them: | T: (none)
an9.38:8.4 | S: ‘They’re included in the world, and haven’t yet left the world.’ | T: (none)
an9.38:8.5 | S: And I also say this: | T: (none)
an9.38:8.6 | S: ‘They’re included in the world, and haven’t yet left the world.’ | T: (none)
an9.38:9.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who enters and remains in the dimension of infinite consciousness. … | T: the infinitude of consciousness,
an9.38:9.2 | S: the dimension of nothingness … | T: nothingness,
an9.38:9.3 | S: the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. | T: and neither perception nor non-perception.]
an9.38:9.4 | S: This is called a mendicant who, having gone to the end of the world, meditates at the end of the world. | T: (none)
an9.38:9.5 | S: Others say of them: | T: (none)
an9.38:9.6 | S: ‘They’re included in the world, and haven’t yet left the world.’ | T: (none)
an9.38:9.7 | S: And I also say this: | T: (none)
an9.38:9.8 | S: ‘They’re included in the world, and haven’t yet left the world.’ | T: (none)
an9.38:10.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, going totally beyond the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, enters and remains in the cessation of perception and feeling. And, having seen with wisdom, their defilements come to an end. | T: “And further, with the complete transcending of the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, he enters & remains in the cessation of perception & feeling. And as he sees (that) with discernment, effluents are completely ended.
an9.38:10.2 | S: This is called a mendicant who, having gone to the end of the world, meditates at the end of the world. And they’ve crossed over clinging to the world.” | T: This is called a monk who, coming to the end of the cosmos, remains at the end of the cosmos, having crossed over attachment in the cosmos.”
an9.38:10.3 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.41
an9.41:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Mallas, near the Mallian town named Uruvelakappa. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Mallans near a Mallan town named Uruvelakappa.
an9.41:1.2 | S: Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Uruvelakappa for alms. | T: Then early in the morning the Blessed One—having adjusted his lower robe and carrying his bowl & outer robe—went into Uruvelakappa for alms.
an9.41:1.3 | S: Then, after the meal, on his return from almsround, he addressed Venerable Ānanda, | T: Having gone into Uruvelakappa for alms, after his meal, on his return from his alms round, he said to Ven. Ānanda,
an9.41:1.4 | S: “Ānanda, you stay right here, while I plunge deep into the Great Wood for the day’s meditation.” | T: “Stay right here, Ānanda, while I go into the Great Forest for the day’s abiding.”
an9.41:1.5 | S: “Yes, sir,” Ānanda replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” Ven. Ānanda responded to him.
an9.41:1.6 | S: Then the Buddha plunged deep into the Great Wood and sat at the root of a tree for the day’s meditation. | T: Then the Blessed One went into the Great Forest and sat down at the root of a certain tree for the day’s abiding.
an9.41:2.1 | S: The householder Tapussa went up to Venerable Ānanda, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then Tapussa the householder went to Ven. Ānanda and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to Ven. Ānanda:
an9.41:3.1 | S: “Honorable Ānanda, we are laypeople who enjoy sensual pleasures. We like sensual pleasures, we love them and take joy in them. | T: “Venerable Ānanda, sir, we are householders who indulge in sensuality, delight in sensuality, enjoy sensuality, rejoice in sensuality.
an9.41:3.2 | S: But renunciation seems like an abyss. | T: For us—indulging in sensuality, delighting in sensuality, enjoying sensuality, rejoicing in sensuality—renunciation seems like a sheer drop-off.
an9.41:3.3 | S: I have heard that in this teaching and training there are very young mendicants whose minds leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in renunciation. They see it as peaceful. | T: Yet I’ve heard that in this Dhamma & Vinaya the hearts of the very young monks leap up at renunciation, grow confident, steadfast, & released, seeing it as peace.
an9.41:3.4 | S: Renunciation is the dividing line between the multitude and the mendicants in this teaching and training.” | T: So right here is where this Dhamma & Vinaya is contrary to the great mass of people: i.e., (this issue of) renunciation.”
an9.41:4.1 | S: “Householder, we should see the Buddha about this matter. | T: “This calls for a talk, householder.
an9.41:4.2 | S: Come, let’s go to the Buddha and inform him about this. | T: Let’s go see the Blessed One. Let’s approach him and, on arrival, tell him this matter.
an9.41:4.3 | S: As he answers, so we’ll remember it.” | T: However he explains it to us, we will bear it in mind.”
an9.41:5.1 | S: “Yes, sir,” replied Tapussa. | T: “As you say, sir,” Tapussa the householder responded to Ven. Ānanda.
an9.41:5.2 | S: Then Ānanda together with Tapussa went to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. Ānanda told him what had happened. | T: Then Ven. Ānanda, together with Tapussa the householder, went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there Ven. Ānanda said to the Blessed One:
an9.41:6.1 | P: “Ayaṁ, bhante, tapusso gahapati evamāha: | T: “Tapussa the householder, here, has said to me,
an9.41:6.2 | P: ‘mayaṁ, bhante ānanda, gihī kāmabhogino kāmārāmā kāmaratā kāmasammuditā, tesaṁ no, bhante, amhākaṁ gihīnaṁ kāmabhogīnaṁ kāmārāmānaṁ kāmaratānaṁ kāmasammuditānaṁ papāto viya khāyati, yadidaṁ nekkhammaṁ’. | T: ‘Venerable Ānanda, sir, we are householders who indulge in sensuality, delight in sensuality, enjoy sensuality, rejoice in sensuality. For us—indulging in sensuality, delighting in sensuality, enjoying sensuality, rejoicing in sensuality—renunciation seems like a sheer drop-off.
an9.41:6.3 | P: Sutaṁ metaṁ, bhante, ‘imasmiṁ dhammavinaye daharānaṁ daharānaṁ bhikkhūnaṁ nekkhamme cittaṁ pakkhandati pasīdati santiṭṭhati vimuccati etaṁ santanti passato. | T: Yet I’ve heard that in this Dhamma & Vinaya the hearts of the very young monks leap up at renunciation, grow confident, steadfast, & released, seeing it as peace.
an9.41:6.4 | P: Tayidaṁ, bhante, imasmiṁ dhammavinaye bhikkhūnaṁ bahunā janena visabhāgo yadidaṁ nekkhamman’”ti. | T: So right here is where this Dhamma & Vinaya is contrary to the great mass of people: i.e., (this issue of) renunciation.’”
an9.41:7.1 | S: “That’s so true, Ānanda! That’s so true! | T: “So it is, Ānanda. So it is.
an9.41:7.2 | S: Before my awakening—when I was still unawakened but intent on awakening—I too thought, | T: Even I myself, before my self-awakening, when I was still an unawakened bodhisatta, thought:
an9.41:7.3 | S: ‘Renunciation is good! Seclusion is good!’ | T: ‘Renunciation is good. Seclusion is good.’
an9.41:7.4 | S: But my mind did not leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in renunciation. I didn’t see it as peaceful. | T: But my heart didn’t leap up at renunciation, didn’t grow confident, steadfast, or released, (though) seeing it as peace.
an9.41:7.5 | S: Then I thought, | T: The thought occurred to me:
an9.41:7.6 | S: ‘What is the cause, what is the reason why my mind does not leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in renunciation? Why don’t I see it as peaceful?’ | T: ‘What is the cause, what is the reason, why my heart doesn’t leap up at renunciation, doesn’t grow confident, steadfast, or released, (though) seeing it as peace?’
an9.41:7.7 | S: Then I thought, | T: Then the thought occurred to me:
an9.41:7.8 | S: ‘I haven’t seen the drawbacks of sensual pleasures, and so I haven’t cultivated that. I haven’t realized the benefits of renunciation, and so I haven’t developed that. | T: ‘I haven’t seen the drawback of sensuality; I haven’t pursued (that theme). I haven’t understood the reward of renunciation; I haven’t familiarized myself with it.
an9.41:7.9 | S: That’s why my mind does not leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in renunciation. And it’s why I don’t see it as peaceful.’ | T: That’s why my heart doesn’t leap up at renunciation, doesn’t grow confident, steadfast, or released, (though) seeing it as peace.’
an9.41:7.10 | S: Then I thought, | T: [1] “Then the thought occurred to me:
an9.41:7.11 | S: ‘Suppose that, seeing the drawbacks of sensual pleasures, I were to cultivate that. And suppose that, realizing the benefits of renunciation, I were to develop that. | T: ‘If, having seen the drawback of sensuality, I were to pursue that theme; and if, having understood the reward of renunciation, I were to familiarize myself with it,
an9.41:7.12 | S: It’s possible that my mind would leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in renunciation. And I would see it as peaceful.’ | T: there’s the possibility that my heart would leap up at renunciation, grow confident, steadfast, & released, seeing it as peace.’
an9.41:7.13 | S: And so, after some time, I saw the drawbacks of sensual pleasures and cultivated that, and I realized the benefits of renunciation and developed that. | T: “So at a later time, having seen the drawback of sensuality, I pursued that theme; having understood the reward of renunciation, I familiarized myself with it.
an9.41:7.14 | S: Then my mind leapt forth, gained confidence, settled down, and became decided in renunciation. I saw it as peaceful. | T: My heart leaped up at renunciation, grew confident, steadfast, & released, seeing it as peace.
an9.41:7.15 | S: And so, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, I entered and remained in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: Then, quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities, I entered & remained in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation.
an9.41:7.16 | S: While I was in that meditation, perception and focus accompanied by sensual pleasures beset me, and that was an affliction for me. | T: “As I remained there, I was beset with attention to perceptions dealing with sensuality. That was an affliction for me.
an9.41:7.17 | S: Suppose a happy person were to experience pain; that would be an affliction for them. | T: Just as pain arises as an affliction for a healthy person,
an9.41:7.18 | S: In the same way, when perception and focus accompanied by sensual pleasures beset me, that was an affliction for me. | T: even so the attention to perceptions dealing with sensuality that beset me was an affliction for me.
an9.41:8.1 | S: Then I thought, | T: [2] “The thought occurred to me:
an9.41:8.2 | S: ‘Why don’t I, as the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled … enter and remain in the second absorption?’ | T: ‘What if, with the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, I were to enter & remain in the second jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of concentration, unification of awareness free from directed thought & evaluation—internal assurance.’
an9.41:8.3 | S: But my mind did not leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in not placing the mind. I didn’t see it as peaceful. | T: But my heart didn’t leap up at being without directed thought, didn’t grow confident, steadfast, or released, (though) seeing it as peace.
an9.41:8.4 | S: Then I thought, | T: The thought occurred to me:
an9.41:8.5 | S: ‘What is the cause, what is the reason why my mind does not leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in not placing the mind? Why don’t I see it as peaceful?’ | T: ‘What is the cause, what is the reason, why my heart doesn’t leap up at being without directed thought, doesn’t grow confident, steadfast, or released, (though) seeing it as peace?’
an9.41:8.6 | S: Then I thought, | T: Then the thought occurred to me:
an9.41:8.7 | S: ‘I haven’t seen the drawbacks of placing the mind, and so I haven’t cultivated that. I haven’t realized the benefits of not placing the mind, and so I haven’t developed that. | T: ‘I haven’t seen the drawback of directed thought; I haven’t pursued that theme. I haven’t understood the reward of being without directed thought; I haven’t familiarized myself with it.
an9.41:8.8 | S: That’s why my mind does not leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in not placing the mind. And it’s why I don’t see it as peaceful.’ | T: That’s why my heart doesn’t leap up at being without directed thought, doesn’t grow confident, steadfast, or released, (though) seeing it as peace.’
an9.41:8.9 | S: Then I thought, | T: “Then the thought occurred to me:
an9.41:8.10 | S: ‘Suppose that, seeing the drawbacks of placing the mind, I were to cultivate that. And suppose that, realizing the benefits of not placing the mind, I were to develop that. It’s possible that my mind would leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in not placing the mind. And I would see it as peaceful.’ | T: ‘If, having seen the drawback of directed thought, I were to pursue that theme; and if, having understood the reward of being without directed thought, I were to familiarize myself with it, there’s the possibility that my heart would leap up at being without directed thought, grow confident, steadfast, & released, seeing it as peace.’
an9.41:8.11 | S: And so, after some time, I saw the drawbacks of placing the mind and cultivated that, and I realized the benefits of not placing the mind and developed that. | T: “So at a later time, having seen the drawback of directed thought, I pursued that theme; having understood the reward of being without directed thought, I familiarized myself with it.
an9.41:8.12 | S: Then my mind did leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in not placing the mind. I saw it as peaceful. | T: My heart leaped up at being without directed thought, grew confident, steadfast, & released, seeing it as peace.
an9.41:8.13 | S: And so, as the placing of the mind and keeping it connected were stilled … I entered and remained in the second absorption. | T: With the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, I entered & remained in the second jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of concentration, unification of awareness free from directed thought & evaluation—internal assurance.
an9.41:8.14 | S: While I was in that meditation, perception and focus accompanied by placing the mind beset me, and that was an affliction for me. | T: “As I remained there, I was beset with attention to perceptions dealing with directed thought. That was an affliction for me.
an9.41:8.15 | S: Suppose a happy person were to experience pain; that would be an affliction for them. | T: Just as pain arises as an affliction for a healthy person,
an9.41:8.16 | S: In the same way, when perception and focus accompanied by placing the mind and keeping it connected beset me, that was an affliction for me. | T: even so the attention to perceptions dealing with directed thought that beset me was an affliction for me.
an9.41:9.1 | S: Then I thought, | T: [3] “The thought occurred to me:
an9.41:9.2 | S: ‘Why don’t I, with the fading away of rapture, enter and remain in the third absorption, where I will meditate with equanimity, mindful and aware, experiencing for themselves the bliss of which the noble ones declare, “Equanimous and mindful, one meditates in bliss”?’ | T: ‘What if, with the fading of rapture, I were to remain equanimous, mindful, & alert, to sense pleasure with the body, and to enter & remain in the third jhāna, of which the noble ones declare, “Equanimous & mindful, he has a pleasant abiding”?’
an9.41:9.3 | S: But my mind did not leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in freedom from rapture. I didn’t see it as peaceful. | T: But my heart didn’t leap up at being without rapture, didn’t grow confident, steadfast, or released, (though) seeing it as peace.…
an9.41:9.4 | S: Then I thought, | T: (none)
an9.41:9.5 | S: ‘What is the cause, what is the reason why my mind does not leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in freedom from rapture? Why don’t I see it as peaceful?’ | T: (none)
an9.41:9.6 | S: Then I thought, | T: (none)
an9.41:9.7 | S: ‘I haven’t seen the drawbacks of rapture, and so I haven’t cultivated that. I haven’t realized the benefits of freedom from rapture, and so I haven’t developed that. | T: (none)
an9.41:9.8 | S: That’s why my mind does not leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in freedom from rapture. And it’s why I don’t see it as peaceful.’ | T: (none)
an9.41:9.9 | S: Then I thought, | T: (none)
an9.41:9.10 | S: ‘Suppose that, seeing the drawbacks of rapture, I were to cultivate that. And suppose that, realizing the benefits of freedom from rapture, I were to develop that. It’s possible that my mind would leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in being free from rapture. And I would see it as peaceful.’ | T: (none)
an9.41:9.11 | S: And so, after some time, I saw the drawbacks of rapture and cultivated that, and I realized the benefits of freedom from rapture and developed that. | T: So at a later time, having seen the drawback of rapture, I pursued that theme; having understood the reward of being without rapture, I familiarized myself with it.
an9.41:9.12 | S: Then my mind leapt forth, gained confidence, settled down, and became decided in freedom from rapture. I saw it as peaceful. | T: My heart leaped up at being without rapture, grew confident, steadfast, & released, seeing it as peace.
an9.41:9.13 | S: And so, with the fading away of rapture … I entered and remained in the third absorption. | T: With the fading of rapture, I remained equanimous, mindful, & alert, sensed pleasure with the body, and entered & remained in the third jhāna, of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous & mindful, he has a pleasant abiding.’
an9.41:9.14 | S: While I was in that meditation, perception and focus accompanied by rapture beset me, and that was an affliction for me. | T: “As I remained there, I was beset with attention to perceptions dealing with rapture. That was an affliction for me.
an9.41:9.15 | S: Suppose a happy person were to experience pain; that would be an affliction for them. | T: Just as pain arises as an affliction for a healthy person,
an9.41:9.16 | S: In the same way, when perception and focus accompanied by rapture beset me, that was an affliction for me. | T: even so the attention to perceptions dealing with rapture that beset me was an affliction for me.
an9.41:10.1 | S: Then I thought, | T: [4] “The thought occurred to me:
an9.41:10.2 | S: ‘Why don’t I, with the giving up of pleasure and pain, and the disappearance of former happiness and sadness, enter and remain in the fourth absorption, without pleasure or pain, with pure equanimity and mindfulness?’ | T: ‘What if, with the abandoning of pleasure & pain—as with the earlier disappearance of elation & distress—I were to enter & remain in the fourth jhāna: purity of equanimity & mindfulness, neither-pleasure-nor-pain?’
an9.41:10.3 | S: But my mind did not leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in being without pleasure and pain. I didn’t see it as peaceful. | T: But my heart didn’t leap up at being without the pleasure of equanimity, didn’t grow confident, steadfast, or released, (though) seeing it as peace.…
an9.41:10.4 | S: Then I thought, | T: (none)
an9.41:10.5 | S: ‘What is the cause, what is the reason why my mind does not leap forth, gain confidence, settle down, and become decided in being without pleasure and pain? Why don’t I see it as peaceful?’ | T: (none)
an9.41:10.6 | S: Then I thought, | T: (none)
an9.41:10.7 | S: ‘I haven’t seen the drawbacks of bliss with equanimity, and so I haven’t cultivated that. I haven’t realized the benefits of being without pleasure and pain, and so I haven’t developed that. | T: (none)

